.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_FULL 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_full \- declare and create an ordered map with custom allocation and comparison
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_full(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs,
                     custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map. That map takes its
memory from mmgmt_procs and orders its keys with custom_comparison_proc.

This is the widest of the construct macros. cbmap_construct(3) is this
macro with the default allocator and the default order.
cbmap_construct_mp(3) gives only an allocator, and cbmap_construct_cc(3)
gives only a comparison procedure. Each of the two arguments can be NULL,
which selects the matching default. This one macro therefore covers every
combination.

The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails.
Use cbmap_init_full(3) on a handle that you declared before, when the caller
must handle a failure and the failure must not be fatal.
cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3) is the same macro with a cleanup attribute.
That attribute destroys the map at the exit of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key. It also tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it.
                        The library never gives it to the container
mmgmt_procs             Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the
                        default allocator
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key. It returns a positive value when
the first key orders after the second key. It returns zero when the map must
treat the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update and not an
insert. The procedure therefore defines key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t. That order must not
change while the map holds elements. The same pair of keys must always
compare in the same way. A comparison in the opposite direction must give
the opposite sign. The order must also be transitive. An inconsistent
procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree. After that, the map
cannot find a key that it holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths. It can only read a
width that key_t fixes. cbmap_create_full(3) describes the default orders
that a procedure replaces. That page also describes the order that a struct
key type gets without a procedure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must not be NULL when the caller
gives one. The map copies the procedures into itself. The procedures and
the comparison procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
Every tree node that the map allocates comes from these procedures.
Everything that the map frees goes back to them
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order that the
map got. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3). Use cbmap_construct_full_scoped(3)
if you want the exit of the scope to destroy it instead
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that receives the handle therefore needs cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};
int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b);

cbmap_construct_full(releases, version, char *, &procs, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
